====================================================================
DOMAIN: Contemporary Philosophy of History -- Narrative Turn and Anti-Realism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-12
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 7, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The narrativist or "linguistic turn" in philosophy of history emerged from the
1960s onward as a challenge to both positivist explanatory models and traditional
speculative philosophy of history. Its central claim is that historical accounts
do not merely describe the past but narratively constitute it: historians impose
literary structures (emplotment, trope, genre) onto events whose significance is
not given by the past itself. Hayden White's Metahistory (1973) launched the
debate by arguing that the nineteenth-century master historians (Michelet, Ranke,
Tocqueville, Burckhardt) and philosophers (Hegel, Marx, Nietzsche, Croce) each
operated with an underlying "metahistorical" tropological code (metaphor, metonymy,
synecdoche, irony) that determined their emplotment strategies (romance, tragedy,
comedy, satire) and their modes of formal argument (formist, organicist, mechanistic,
contextualist). The implication is devastating for speculative history: if even
"empirical" history is structured by literary trope, then grand narrative schemes
(providential, dialectical, progressive) are simply emplotment strategies operating
at the highest scale, with no privileged cognitive status. Frank Ankersmit radicalized
this insight through his concept of "narrative substance"---historical narratives are
not true or false but stylistically display an irreducible surplus beyond the
statements they contain---and later through his "sublime historical experience"
thesis, which relocates historical knowledge in a pre-linguistic, traumatic
rupture between past and present. Keith Jenkins and Alun Munslow pushed to strong
constructivist anti-realism: history is always history-as-written, shaped by the
historian's present concerns, and has no epistemic access to "the past as it was."
Frank Kermode's analysis of narrative closure (The Sense of an Ending, 1966) offered
an allied argument from literary theory: the "sense of an ending" is a fiction humans
impose on contingent temporal experience; for speculative history this means that
teleological narratives smuggle in a literary device that is not epistemically
warranted. Paul Ricoeur's Time and Narrative (1983-1985) offered a partial and
philosophically sophisticated rehabilitation: narrative gives historical experience
coherence via "emplotment" (drawn from Aristotle's muthos), but this coherence is
heuristic rather than metaphysically necessary, and narrative does not collapse into
relativism because its claims are constrained by testimony and the "documentary
pact" with evidence. Dominick LaCapra complicated the picture from a psychoanalytic
direction: the historian's relationship to traumatic historical events resists
narrativization; "working through" trauma without resolving it into seamless narrative
is both ethically required and theoretically significant.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly addresses whether speculative philosophy of history survives the
narrativist challenge. White's tropology effectively reclassifies speculative history
as "emplotment writ large"---a literary-rhetorical activity with aesthetic rather than
cognitive warrant. Jenkins and Munslow's anti-realism denies speculative history any
epistemic grip on what-actually-happened or what history as a whole means. Ricoeur's
partial rehabilitation is the most important resource for a chastened defense of
speculative claims, showing that narrative coherence need not entail teleological
necessity. Ankersmit's "sublime experience" thesis is ambivalent: it could rehabilitate
an experiential dimension of speculative history or dissolve it into aesthetic encounter.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No recent monograph-length defense of speculative philosophy of history against the
narrativist critique was located (post-2010). The recent "new historical realism"
debate (Mitrovic, Forland) has not yet produced a work specifically rehabilitating
speculative claims. LaCapra's Rethinking Intellectual History (1983) and History and
Criticism (1985) were not indexed by CrossRef and could not be included. White 1973,
Ankersmit 1983, and Ricoeur 1984 were marked INCOMPLETE (no abstract in databases)
but are included as foundational primary texts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The key tension for synthesis is between White/Ankersmit (narrativism dissolves
speculative history into aesthetics or rhetoric) and Ricoeur (narrative is the proper
medium for historical coherence without metaphysical teleology). Jenkins/Munslow
represent the terminus of the anti-realist trajectory. The Kuukkanen 2025 review and
Hubalek-Kowalewski 2025 article provide current state-of-play on realism/anti-realism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Narrativism/tropology (White, Ankersmit): 5 papers -- emplotment is literary, not epistemic
- Strong anti-realism (Jenkins, Munslow): 2 papers -- history as writing, no access to the past
- Ricoeurian rehabilitation: 3 papers -- narrative coherence without teleological necessity
- Critical/diagnostic approaches (Lorenz, Zammito, Kuukkanen): 3 papers -- critiques of both sides
- Recent realism/naturalism debate: 2 papers -- post-anti-realist paths forward
====================================================================
}

@book{white1973metahistory,
  author = {White, Hayden},
  title = {Metahistory: The Historical Imagination in Nineteenth-Century Europe},
  year = {1973},
  publisher = {Johns Hopkins University Press},
  address = {Baltimore},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: White argues that the great nineteenth-century historians and
  philosophers of history each operated with a hidden "metahistorical" code
  combining a dominant literary trope (metaphor, metonymy, synecdoche, irony),
  an emplotment mode (romance, tragedy, comedy, satire), a formal explanatory
  argument (formist, organicist, mechanistic, contextualist), and an ideological
  implication (anarchist, radical, conservative, liberal). These choices are made
  on aesthetic and moral grounds prior to empirical inquiry, not determined by
  the evidence.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for the narrativist challenge to
  speculative history: if Hegel's dialectical narrative is identifiable as
  "comedy" emplotted by synecdoche, and Marx's as "tragedy" emplotted by
  metonymy, then speculative history is not a cognitive enterprise but a
  poetic one. The tropological framework collapses the distinction between
  speculative and empirical history into a common literary-rhetorical space,
  undercutting both's claim to cognitive privilege.

  POSITION: White's tropology represents the strongest literary-turn challenge
  to speculative history, classifying it as emplotment at maximal scale.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, tropology, emplotment, speculative-history-critique, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{ankersmit1983narrative,
  author = {Ankersmit, F. R.},
  title = {Narrative Logic: A Semantic Analysis of the Historian's Language},
  year = {1983},
  publisher = {Martinus Nijhoff},
  address = {The Hague},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ankersmit introduces the concept of "narrative substance" to
  characterize historical narratives: unlike individual statements (which can
  be verified against evidence), the narrative as a whole proposes an
  interpretation of a historical period that has no direct referent in the past.
  Narrative substances are evaluated aesthetically and stylistically, not truth-
  conditionally; they are more like artworks than propositions.

  RELEVANCE: The narrative substance doctrine is pivotal for assessing speculative
  history's epistemic standing. If even ordinary historical narratives lack
  truth-conditions at the macro-level, speculative philosophies of history---which
  operate at the level of civilizational or universal wholes---are even further
  removed from verification. The book develops the anti-representationalist logic
  that culminates in Ankersmit's later claim that historical writing is aesthetic
  display, not knowledge.

  POSITION: This initiates Ankersmit's anti-representationalist narrativism,
  related to White but more analytically grounded in philosophy of language.
  Cross-domain connection to Domain 2 (Danto's narrative sentences).
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, narrative-substance, Ankersmit, speculative-history-critique, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{ricoeur1984time,
  author = {Ricoeur, Paul},
  title = {Time and Narrative, Volume 1},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {University of Chicago Press},
  address = {Chicago},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ricoeur argues that human experience of time is constituted through
  narrative: the act of emplotment (muthos in Aristotle's sense) is the fundamental
  operation by which temporal succession is transformed into meaningful historical
  or fictional experience. History and fiction are brought together under a single
  "narrative understanding"; history is distinguished from fiction not by its narrative
  structure but by its "documentary pact"---its constraints from evidence and testimony.
  Narrative identity does not entail teleological necessity: a plot unifies without
  predetermining.

  RELEVANCE: Ricoeur's architecture is the most philosophically sophisticated attempt
  to preserve narrative coherence in history without collapsing into either anti-realism
  (White, Ankersmit) or speculative teleology. The claim that emplotment "follows" events
  rather than "legislating" them provides a partial rehabilitation: speculative history
  errs not in using narrative but in treating narrative necessity as ontological necessity.
  This is the key resource for a "chastened" speculative philosophy of history. Cross-domain
  connection to Domain 5 (Ricoeur).

  POSITION: Partial rehabilitation of narrative in history against both positivist
  explanatory models and anti-realist dissolution; moderate narrativism.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ricoeur, emplotment, narrative-rehabilitation, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{ankersmit2001historical,
  author = {Ankersmit, F. R.},
  title = {Historical Representation},
  year = {2002},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  address = {Stanford},
  doi = {10.1515/9781503619029},
  abstract = {This book fully recognizes the aestheticism inherent in historical writing while acknowledging its claim to satisfy the demands of rational and scientific inquiry. Focusing on the notion of representation and on the necessity of distinguishing between representation and description, it argues that the traditional semantic apparatus of meaning, truth, and reference that we use for description must be redefined if we are to understand properly the nature of historical writing. The author shows that historical representation is essentially aesthetic, though its adequacy can be discussed rationally. He defines the criteria for representational adequacy, and examines the relationship between these criteria and value judgments. He also investigates the historicist conception of historical writing and the notions of identity and narrativity. This investigation takes place against the backdrop of the ideas of four of the most influential contemporary historical theorists: Erich Auerbach, Arthur Danto, Hayden White, and Jorn Rüsen. The book aims to identify and to explore for historical theory the juste milieu between the extravagances of the literary approach to historical writing and the narrow-mindedness of empiricists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ankersmit develops a systematic anti-representationalist philosophy
  of history: historical representations (narratives, interpretations) cannot
  be understood as descriptions of the past because they introduce a new "level
  of meaning" irreducible to the statements they contain. The historical text
  represents not by picturing but by displaying a perspective; truth-talk applies
  only to individual statements, not to the interpretive whole. The distinction
  between "style" and "content" becomes crucial: style carries the irreducible
  interpretive commitment.

  RELEVANCE: This is Ankersmit's most systematic treatment of why speculative
  history---understood as a macro-level interpretation of history as a whole---
  cannot be assessed as true or false but only as more or less illuminating,
  aesthetically compelling, or useful. The implications for rehabilitating
  speculative claims are bleak: representation without correspondence removes
  the epistemic grounding speculative history requires.

  POSITION: Strong anti-representationalism that parallels Jenkins/Munslow but
  from within an analytical philosophical tradition; connects to aesthetics of
  history.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, anti-representationalism, Ankersmit, historical-representation, High}
}

@book{ankersmit2005sublime,
  author = {Ankersmit, F. R.},
  title = {Sublime Historical Experience},
  year = {2005},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  address = {Stanford},
  doi = {10.1515/9781503624597},
  abstract = {Why are we interested in history at all? Why do we feel the need to distinguish between past and present? In this book, the author argues that the past originates from an experience of rupture separating past and present. Think of the radical rupture with Europe's past that was effected by the French and the Industrial Revolutions. Sublime Historical Experience investigates how the notion of sublime historical experience complicates and challenges existing conceptions of language, truth, and knowledge. These experiences of rupture are paradoxical since they involve both the separation of past and present and, at the same time, the effort to overcome this separation in terms of historical knowledge. The experience unites feelings of loss/pain with those of love/satisfaction, and thus is in agreement with how sublime experience is ordinarily defined. The experience is also precognitive since it precedes (the possibility of) historical knowledge. As such it is a challenge to traditional conceptions of the relationship between experience and truth or language. It compels us to disconnect the notions of experience and truth.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ankersmit shifts from narrative to "experience" as the key
  category: the paradigmatic historical experience is "sublime"---a traumatic
  rupture in which the historian momentarily loses the present and is immersed
  in a vanished past, an experience that is pre-linguistic, non-conceptual,
  and beyond narrative. This experience is the ultimate ground of historical
  knowledge but cannot itself be articulated within any narrative framework.

  RELEVANCE: Ankersmit's turn to sublime experience has a paradoxical bearing
  on speculative history. On one hand, it rehabilitates a direct encounter with
  the past that bypasses the constructivist problem. On the other hand, the
  pre-linguistic, traumatic character of this experience is precisely what
  speculative history's grand narratives suppress and domesticate. This creates
  a double critique: speculative history fails both representationally (narrative
  substances don't refer) and experientially (the past resists narrative integration).

  POSITION: Late Ankersmit, moving from narrative anti-representationalism to
  experiential aesthetics of history; ambivalent for rehabilitation of speculative claims.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, sublime-experience, Ankersmit, aesthetics-of-history, High}
}

@book{munslow1997deconstructing,
  author = {Munslow, Alun},
  title = {Deconstructing History},
  year = {1997},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  address = {London},
  doi = {10.4324/9780203131589},
  abstract = {In Deconstructing History, Alun Munslow examines history in the postmodern age. He provides an introduction to the debates and issues of postmodernist history. He also surveys the latest research into the relationship between the past, history and historical practice as well as forwarding his own challenging theories. The book discusses issues of both empiricist and deconstruction positions and considers the arguments of major proponents of both stances. It includes an examination of the character of historical evidence, exploration of the role of historians, discussion of the failure of traditional historical methods, chapters on Hayden White and Michel Foucault, an evaluation of the importance of historical narrative. He argues that instead of beginning with the past, history should begin with its representation by historians.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Munslow categorizes approaches to history along a spectrum from
  "reconstructionism" (naive realism: history recovers the past through sources)
  through "constructionism" (history builds interpretations from evidence via theory)
  to "deconstructionism" (history is a written discourse that cannot transcend the
  literary-linguistic medium: the past as it was is inaccessible). Munslow advocates
  the deconstructionist position, drawing on White, Derrida, and poststructuralist
  theory.

  RELEVANCE: Munslow's framework explicitly targets speculative history as the extreme
  form of constructionism (imposing prior theoretical frameworks on the past) whose
  failure to acknowledge the literary nature of historical writing is a fundamental
  self-deception. Deconstructing History is the most systematic presentation of the
  strong anti-realist case against any philosophy of history---speculative or analytical.

  POSITION: Strong postmodern anti-realism; alongside Jenkins, represents the terminus
  of the narrativist challenge to speculative history's cognitive claims.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, anti-realism, postmodern-history, Munslow, speculative-history-critique, High}
}

@book{jenkins1991rethinking,
  author = {Jenkins, Keith},
  title = {Re-Thinking History},
  year = {1991},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jenkins argues that history is always a present-oriented discourse:
  historians construct narratives about the past from a particular ideological and
  linguistic position, and there is no neutral access to "the past as it was."
  Drawing on poststructuralism, Jenkins concludes that history as a discipline is
  constitutively bound to power and interests; any claim to objective, truthful
  recovery of the past is self-deceptive. The question "what is history for?" is
  irreducibly political, not epistemological.

  RELEVANCE: Jenkins' strong constructivism represents the clearest statement of
  why speculative philosophy of history is doubly mistaken: not only does it
  impose a grand narrative framework (the critique White and Ankersmit make), but
  it presents this framework as a discovery about the past rather than a production
  of the present. Speculative history's claim to reveal history's meaning or
  direction is, on Jenkins' account, a paradigm case of ideological mystification.

  POSITION: Strong postmodern anti-realism; together with Munslow, represents the
  most thoroughgoing rejection of any cognitive ambitions for philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, anti-realism, postmodern-history, Jenkins, speculative-history-critique, Medium}
}

@article{lorenz1998histories,
  author = {Lorenz, Chris},
  title = {Can Histories Be True? Narrativism, Positivism, and the ``Metaphorical Turn''},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1998},
  volume = {37},
  number = {3},
  pages = {309--329},
  doi = {10.1111/0018-2656.00056},
  abstract = {Narrativism, as represented by Hayden White and Frank Ankersmit, can fruitfully be analyzed as an inversion of two brands of positivism. First, narrativist epistemology can be regarded as an inversion of empiricism. Its thesis that narratives function as metaphors which do not possess a cognitive content is built on an empiricist, "picture view" of knowledge. Moreover, all the non-cognitive aspects attributed as such are dependent on this picture theory of knowledge and a picture theory of representation. Most of the epistemological characteristics that White and Ankersmit attribute to historical narratives therefore share the problems of this picture theory. The article's second thesis is that the theories of narrative explanation can also fruitfully be analyzed as inversions of positivist covering-law theory. Ankersmit's brand of narrativism is the most radical in this respect because it posits an opposition between narrative and causal modes of comprehension while simultaneously eliminating causality from narrativist historical understanding. White's brand of narrativism is more of a hybrid in this respect; nevertheless, it can also be fruitfully interpreted as an inversion of covering-law theory, replacing it by an indefinite multitude of explanatory strategies.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lorenz provides a systematic critique of White's narrativism on
  epistemological grounds: the claim that historical narratives are structured by
  literary tropes and modes of emplotment does not entail that they cannot also be
  true or false. Lorenz distinguishes between the "rhetoric" of historical texts
  (how claims are presented) and the "logic" of historical argument (whether claims
  are supported by evidence). White conflates the two; recognizing their independence
  preserves the cognitive dimension of historical writing without naive positivism.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most influential analytical critique of White's tropological
  anti-realism from within philosophy of history. It provides a framework for
  assessing whether speculative history can be criticized on its own cognitive terms
  without collapsing into the "it's all metaphor" dismissal. Lorenz shows that
  narrativism is correct about the presence of rhetorical structures but wrong
  to infer anti-realism from their presence.

  POSITION: Analytical realist critique of narrativism; defends the cognitive dimension
  of historical writing against tropological dissolution.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, narrativism-critique, White, realism-anti-realism-debate, High}
}

@article{zammito1998ankersmit,
  author = {Zammito, John H.},
  title = {Ankersmit's Postmodernist Historiography: The Hyperbole of ``Opacity''},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1998},
  volume = {37},
  number = {3},
  pages = {330--346},
  doi = {10.1111/0018-2656.00057},
  abstract = {Ankersmit's articulation of a postmodern theory of history takes seriously both the strengths of traditional historicism and the right of historians to decide what makes sense for disciplinary practice. That makes him an exemplary interlocutor. Ankersmit proposes a theory of historical "representation" which radicalizes the narrative approach to historiography along the lines of poststructuralist textualism. Against this postmodernism but invoking some of his own arguments, I defend the traditional historicist position. I formulate criticisms of the theory of reference entailed in his notion of "narrative substance," of his master analogy of historiography with modern painting, and finally of his characterization of historical hermeneutics. In each case I find him guilty of the hyperbole which he himself cautions against. While it is true that historical narratives cannot be taken to be transparent, in taking them to be opaque Ankersmit puts himself in an untenable position. Finally, Ankersmit seeks to buttress his theoretical case by an interpretation of the new cultural historical texts of authors like Davis and Ginzburg. While this is a concreteness heartily to be welcomed in philosophers of history, I cannot find his construction of this new school's work plausible.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zammito argues that Ankersmit's narrative substance doctrine is
  incoherent: the claim that historical texts have a "surplus of meaning" beyond
  their constituent statements---a holistic "opacity"---is either trivially true
  (all complex texts do) or self-defeating (it severs any connection between the
  text's claims and the past, making historical knowledge impossible). Ankersmit's
  aestheticization of history is a hyperbolic extension of correct observations
  about historical writing into a general anti-epistemology.

  RELEVANCE: Zammito's critique is crucial for evaluating whether Ankersmit's
  anti-representationalism genuinely undermines speculative history or whether it
  merely redescribes it. If Ankersmit's anti-realism is incoherent, the narrativist
  challenge to speculative history may be weaker than it appears. The article
  appeared alongside Lorenz (1998) as part of a critical reassessment of the
  narrativist turn in the same issue of History and Theory.

  POSITION: Analytical critique of Ankersmit's anti-representationalism; argues
  for a moderate realism about historical narratives.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ankersmit-critique, narrativism, realism, Medium}
}

@article{carter2003telling,
  author = {Carter, Jonathan A.},
  title = {Telling Times: History, Emplotment, and Truth},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {42},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--27},
  doi = {10.1111/1468-2303.00226},
  abstract = {In Time, Narrative, and History, David Carr argues against the narrativist claim that our lived experience does not possess the formal attributes of a story; this conclusion can be reinforced from a semiotic perspective. Our experience is mediated through temporal signs that are used again in the construction of stories. Since signs are social entities from the start, this approach avoids a problem of individualism specific to phenomenology. A semiotic framework is also explicit about a theme Carr handles implicitly: the status of representation. Representation is internal to signification, mediating our experience not just retrospectively but prospectively in the planning and execution of action. A model is presented in which the temporal organization of experience and action is formally coordinated with the temporal organization of narrative. The model is then applied to a historical event: John Batman's attempt in 1835 to purchase land from some Aboriginal tribes around what is now Melbourne. The meaning of this event is not located only in historical writing about it but in the semiotic constitution of the event itself. Changes of meaning emerge from relations between events as new events align with old ones. Finally, a number of contrasts are indicated between this model and proposals made by Arthur C. Danto, Hayden White, F. R. Ankersmit, Fernand Braudel, and Paul Ricoeur.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carter examines how the concept of emplotment---the imposition of
  narrative form on historical events---relates to historical truth. Drawing on
  White and Ricoeur, Carter argues that emplotment is not merely a rhetorical
  decoration but constitutive of how historians make events intelligible; yet this
  does not preclude truth, because emplotment operates under constraints from
  evidence and the "reality of the past." The article develops a nuanced middle
  position between White's aestheticism and naive correspondence theory.

  RELEVANCE: Carter's mediation between White and Ricoeur is directly relevant to
  evaluating whether speculative history's large-scale emplotments can be constrained
  by evidence. If "telling times" is always partly evaluative, speculative history's
  claim to truth is a matter of degree, not kind. The article provides tools for
  distinguishing defensible from indefensible speculative narratives.

  POSITION: Moderate narrativism; preserves truth-conditions for historical emplotment
  against both White's aestheticism and positivist anti-narrativism.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, emplotment, truth, White, Ricoeur, Medium}
}

@article{domanska2009ankersmit,
  author = {Domanska, Ewa},
  title = {Frank Ankersmit: From Narrative to Experience},
  journal = {Rethinking History},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {13},
  number = {2},
  pages = {175--195},
  doi = {10.1080/13642520902833809},
  abstract = {This paper analyzes key issues in the work of Frank Ankersmit: narrative, representation and sublime historical experience. It argues that his recent turn to experience marks a shift from an interest in narrative and the textual dimension of the past to an examination of the notion of an experience about the past. It suggests that although Ankersmit is usually associated with postmodernist avangardism in historical theory (narrativism, constructivism), as can be seen in his theory of historical representation, his understanding of the concept of historical experience and the sublime can be seen as a return to a traditional Romantic view of immediate experience. Thus, although Ankersmit had pushed historical theory beyond the linguistic turn, his most recent work can be understood as a return combined with an Enlightenment analysis of that experience.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Domanska traces Ankersmit's intellectual development from his early
  narrativism (narrative substances, opposition to correspondence theory) through
  his "experiential turn" to the concept of "sublime historical experience." The
  article shows how Ankersmit moved from philosophy of language to phenomenology,
  arguing that the motivation for this shift was the inadequacy of linguistic
  models to capture the historian's actual relationship to the past---which Ankersmit
  increasingly saw as pre-linguistic and traumatic.

  RELEVANCE: By mapping Ankersmit's trajectory, Domanska helps locate the precise
  point at which his anti-representationalism transitions from threatening speculative
  history (narrative without reference) to potentially rehabilitating something
  like a direct historical encounter. The article is essential for understanding
  whether Ankersmit's later work opens or closes a space for speculative philosophy.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical mapping of Ankersmit's philosophy; sympathetic
  reconstruction of the narrative-to-experience trajectory.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ankersmit, sublime-experience, narrative-substance, Medium}
}

@article{jansen2015time,
  author = {Jansen, Harry},
  title = {Time, Narrative, and Fiction: The Uneasy Relationship Between Ricoeur and a Heterogeneous Temporality},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {54},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--24},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.10738},
  abstract = {In Time and Narrative, Paul Ricoeur confirms the relationship between time experience and how it is epitomized in a narrative by investigating historiography and fiction. Regarding fiction, he explores temporality in three "novels of time": Mrs. Dalloway by Virginia Woolf, The Magic Mountain by Thomas Mann, and A la recherche du temps perdu by Marcel Proust. Ricoeur perceives the temporalities as homogeneous; however, in my view, the novels contain at least three different temporalities. Mann seeks a new temporality by ironizing a romantic time of rise and fall and Woolf configures a time we can call the simultaneity of the dissimultaneous. In his analysis of Proust's A la recherche du temps perdu, Ricoeur explicitly dismisses a Bergsonian approach to temporality. In my opinion, Bergson defends a heterogeneous time that is apparent in Proust's novel.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jansen argues that Ricoeur's Time and Narrative model presupposes
  a homogeneous temporality that does not adequately capture the heterogeneous,
  non-linear temporal structures found in both fiction and actual historical
  experience. Drawing on phenomenological sources, Jansen contends that Ricoeur's
  emplotment model---which requires a narrative arc from beginning through middle
  to end---systematically privileges closure over the genuinely open temporal
  character of lived experience.

  RELEVANCE: This critique is directly relevant to the speculative history question
  because speculative history typically imposes a direction and closure on historical
  time. If even Ricoeur's moderate emplotment model smuggles in an unjustified
  temporal homogeneity, speculative history's teleological structures face a deeper
  phenomenological objection than Ricoeur's partial rehabilitation admits.

  POSITION: Phenomenological critique of Ricoeur's narrativism from within; relevant
  to Domain 5 cross-domain connection.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ricoeur, temporality, emplotment-critique, narrative-closure, Medium}
}

@article{ankersmit2021narrativist,
  author = {Ankersmit, Frank R.},
  title = {A Narrativist Revival?},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {15},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--239},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341456},
  abstract = {Up till the 1980s narrativist philosophers of history were mainly interested in the cognitivist dimension of historical narrative. With Hayden White this interest was exchanged for an exclusive preoccupation with the literary aspects of the historian's narrative representation of the past. However, it may seem that a revival of pre-Whitean narrativist philosophy of history is at hand. Two recent books suggest as much: one by Chiel van den Akker published in 2018 and one more by Paul Roth that came out in 2020. Obviously, a narrativist revival can take two different forms. It may aim at providing pre-Whitean narrativism with a more up-to-date philosophical basis or at guiding it into new directions. It will be argued in this review-essay that the book by Roth mainly does the former, whereas the book by Van den Akker does both.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ankersmit reviews two recent books (van den Akker 2018, Roth 2020)
  that attempt to revive narrativist philosophy of history in a post-Whitean register.
  He distinguishes between "pre-Whitean" narrativism (focused on the cognitive
  content of narratives: Danto, Mink, Gallie) and post-Whitean preoccupation with
  literary form. He argues that van den Akker's approach offers more genuine
  theoretical novelty, while Roth's naturalism largely repackages older positions.
  Ankersmit remains skeptical of any narrativism that restores the cognitive dimension
  while ignoring the aesthetic surplus he identified in narrative substances.

  RELEVANCE: This 2021 essay by Ankersmit himself surveys the current state of
  narrativist philosophy of history and its revival attempts, directly relevant to
  assessing whether speculative history can find any resources in a revived narrativism.
  Ankersmit's continued skepticism about cognitive narrativism reinforces the anti-realist
  challenge. Cross-domain connection to Domain 2 (Danto).

  POSITION: Ankersmit's late assessment of the narrativist tradition; persistent
  anti-cognitivist commitment against revival of realist narrativism.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ankersmit, narrativist-revival, cognitive-narrativism, Medium}
}

@article{kuukkanen2025unwarranted,
  author = {Kuukkanen, Jouni-Matti},
  title = {Unwarranted Confidence: A Critical Review of {The Poverty of Anti-Realism}},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {65},
  number = {2},
  pages = {271--297},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.70025},
  abstract = {The Poverty of Anti-Realism: Critical Perspectives on Postmodernist Philosophy of History, edited by Tor Egil Forland and Branko Mitrovic, celebrates the new dawn of historical realism, which it claims supersedes the erroneous and harmful anti-realism. The volume indeed contributes to reinvigorating the debate surrounding realism and anti-realism and draws attention to a relatively neglected philosophical approach in the philosophy of historiography: realism. However, both the tone and content of this New Historical Realism leave much to be desired. The book displays unwarranted confidence regarding the quality of its content and its grasp of philosophical issues. Many of the essays in this volume exhibit a lack of understanding about the basic issues of realism and anti-realism and are riddled with conceptual confusions. Philosophical notions such as anti-realism, irrealism, postmodernism, idealism, and constructivism are used casually and even interchangeably. Although there are some glimmers of light and more measured approaches in the book, its broad-brush categories are premised on an us-against-them view of the scholarly debate, presenting the rival anti-realistic position simplistically as detrimental, deficient, and wholly objectionable. I end my article with a plea for prudent scholarship and reviewing in the philosophy of historiography and philosophy more generally.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kuukkanen critically reviews an edited volume (Forland and Mitrovic,
  eds.) that claims to celebrate a "new historical realism" overcoming postmodern
  anti-realism. He argues that the volume displays conceptual confusion---treating
  anti-realism, irrealism, constructivism, and postmodernism as interchangeable---
  and that its confident dismissal of anti-realism is epistemically unwarranted.
  Kuukkanen defends a more discriminating approach that acknowledges the genuine
  philosophical insights of anti-realism while avoiding its excesses.

  RELEVANCE: This 2025 article provides the most current mapping of the realism/
  anti-realism debate in philosophy of history, directly bearing on whether a
  rehabilitated speculative history can anchor itself to a realist epistemology.
  Kuukkanen's call for "prudent scholarship" implies that neither strong anti-realism
  nor naive realism provides adequate philosophical tools; the speculative tradition
  needs a more nuanced framework.

  POSITION: Current state-of-the-art critique from a moderate anti-anti-realist
  position; most recent (2025) intervention in the debate.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, realism-anti-realism-debate, Kuukkanen, postmodern-history, recent, Medium}
}

@article{piercey2024ricoeur,
  author = {Piercey, Robert},
  title = {Is {Paul Ricoeur} Still Relevant to the Philosophy of History?},
  journal = {Rethinking History},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {28},
  number = {4},
  pages = {532--557},
  doi = {10.1080/13642529.2024.2431762},
  abstract = {When Paul Ricoeur's Time and Narrative was published, it was hailed as a major contribution to the philosophy of history. In the four decades since, Ricoeur's brand of narrativism has fallen out of favour, and many of the claims he makes about historical narrative have been sharply criticized. Nevertheless, I argue that Ricoeur's narrativism remains a powerful resource for philosophical reflections on the study of history. After reconstructing Ricoeur's narrativism and placing it in its larger context, I discuss two sorts of criticism that have been directed at it: one made by literary theorists such as Monika Fludernik and another made by philosophers such as Jouni-Matti Kuukkanen. I defend Ricoeur from both sorts of criticism, arguing that when his approach is compared to its competitors, it fares remarkably well. I conclude with some suggestions about how philosophers of history might build on Ricoeur's legacy today.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piercey argues that Ricoeur's narrativism---despite falling out of
  fashion in the four decades since Time and Narrative---remains philosophically
  viable when properly understood. He distinguishes Ricoeur's approach from the
  anti-realist versions (White, Ankersmit) and defends it against two lines of
  criticism: from literary theorists (Fludernik) who argue the model is too cognitive,
  and from philosophers (Kuukkanen) who argue it is not scientific enough. Piercey
  concludes that Ricoeur's framework is the most defensible moderate narrativism
  available.

  RELEVANCE: This 2024 assessment directly addresses the question of whether Ricoeur
  provides a rehabilitation path for speculative history against the anti-realist
  challenge. If Ricoeur's moderate narrativism is defensible, speculative history
  can be understood as pursuing a narrative coherence that is constrained but not
  reducible to correspondence. The article also maps the current field of narrativism
  scholarship. Cross-domain connection to Domain 5 (Ricoeur).

  POSITION: Recent (2024) defense of Ricoeurian narrativism as a via media between
  anti-realism and naive realism; directly relevant to rehabilitation question.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, Ricoeur, narrative-rehabilitation, narrativism, recent, Low}
}

@article{hubalek2025naturalizing,
  author = {Hub{\'a}lek, Michal and Kowalewski Jahromi, Piotr},
  title = {Naturalizing the Past(s): Three Rothian Steps Toward a Future Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {19},
  number = {3},
  pages = {319--344},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341568},
  abstract = {In light of the recent efforts to resuscitate a form of historical realism by Branko Mitrovic and others, this essay situates their efforts in between past and present debates not only about historical realism and anti-realism, but also about the scientific status of historiography. In the conviction that we can and should learn from our past, we present a genealogy of the idea of Universal History, starting from Kant, as it is also here where a certain dualism between empirical and philosophical historiography was introduced. Our analysis eventually brings us to the radical rejection of Universal History by Louis Mink, and especially then Paul Roth, who draws substantial epistemological and metaphysical conclusions from this rejection. Roth's work is often considered to be controversial since he makes a case not only for the plurality of historiographical methods, but for the very plurality of pasts, too. So, we show that Roth's often misinterpreted appeals to methodological intersectionality and metaphysical multiplicity must be always understood from within his naturalism. We moreover show that Roth, contra the realists in question, constructively works with the adaptive shifts that the theory and philosophy of history made in the 20th century, namely we identify three Rothian starting points or steps: retrospectivism, irrealism, and the already-mentioned naturalism. So, when taken altogether, our essay points at a possible new path which the future philosophy of history might take as its own -- a path beyond the beaten paths of historical realism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hubalek and Kowalewski Jahromi trace the "new historical realism"
  debate (Mitrovic et al.) and situate Paul Roth's naturalist philosophy of history
  as a constructive alternative. They identify three Rothian "steps": retrospectivism
  (historical knowledge is always constituted from the present), irrealism (no single
  determinate past exists independent of historical framing), and naturalism (historics
  should be continuous with natural science's fallibilism). They argue this framework
  surpasses both strong anti-realism and naive realism.

  RELEVANCE: This 2025 article maps post-narrativist options for philosophy of history.
  A "Rothian" framework would imply that speculative history's claim to know the meaning
  of history as a whole is doubly undermined---by retrospectivism (its framework is
  present-laden) and by irrealism (there is no single determinate past-as-a-whole to
  be known). The article suggests paths beyond the realism/anti-realism impasse, relevant
  to evaluating whether speculative history has any future.

  POSITION: Recent (2025) naturalist/irrealist position post-dating the White/Ankersmit
  tradition; represents current post-narrativist state of the field.
  },
  keywords = {narrativism-anti-realism, realism-anti-realism-debate, naturalism, irrealism, recent, Low}
}
